\par\par \b \uc2  Bank of Israel expected to cut rates 0.3-0.5 pct. \b0\par\par\uc2 455 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Januar 1997\par 12:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TEL AVIV, Jan 27 (Reuter) - The Bank of Israel is expected to announce an interest rate cut of 0.3 to 0.5 percentage point from a rate of 14.7 percent, analysts said on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Israel is scheduled to announce its February monetary programme at 1430 GMT today. \par \par \uc2  "My forecast was previously for an 0.3 point decrease based on the consumer price index," said Jonathan Katz, chief economist for Solid Financial Markets. "Now part of me is leaning towards 0.5 point because of the economic indicators released in recent days." \par \par \uc2  Katz cited the drop in the "S" index of economic performance for November and December, lower industrial production and weak trade figures. \par \par \uc2  "The whole trend in interest rates is now more downward than previously expected," he said. "You're now looking at negative growth, not just a slowdown." \par \par \uc2  Bank of Israel Governor Jacob Frenkel also faces exchange rate pressures. The shekel has been strong and its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -basket rate has been near the bottom of the trading band for more than a week, which has forced the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and inject an estimated $500 million of liquidity into the economy. \par \par \uc2  Eli Nahum, head of research at Zannex Securities, said the market was also looking for a cut of 0.3 to 0.5 point. \par \par \uc2  But he and other analysts said the market is focused less on interest rates than on the unfolding government crisis, declining share prices on Wall Street and concerns that the market is overbought after a nearly uninterrupted rise in share prices since the start of November. \par \par \uc2  Worries about an overbought situation were alleviated by a 3.61 percent plunge in the Mishtanim index of top 100 shares on Sunday. Today the index was up 1.22 percent to 234.75 points in midday trading. \par \par \uc2  The government crisis, however, may continue to shadow the market, Nahum said. \par \par \uc2  The crisis stems from allegations of high-level corruption in the appointment of an attorney-general earlier this month. On Sunday, Israeli police launched an investigation into the matter. \par \par \uc2  Nahum said the affair has sparked worries that Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu's government could fall. \par \par \uc2  "I personally think that (is the reason for the market's nervousness), but a lot of players don't," he said. "Really, the market was looking for a reason to take profits." \par \par \uc2  He said the market might be driven by interest rates if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  opted to hold rates or to cut them by more than 0.5 point. \par \par \uc2  - David Rosenberg, Tel Aviv newsroom, 972-3-537-2211 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt1r0105u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
