\par\par \b \uc2  Israel/News-2: Discount Rate Cut Is Front Page News \b0\par\par\uc2 303 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 1996\par 10:43\par \uc2  Emerging Markets Report \par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1996, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  --Another front page item was the Bank of Israel's decision to cut the discount rate by three-tenths of a percentage point next month to 15.5%. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the government budget deficit was one factor that prevented a deeper cut. Ha'aretz reported that government economic sources are weighing cutting the 1997 budget by 5.9 billion shekels rather than the originally proposed 4.9 billion shekels. \par \par \uc2  Globes, in its front-page commentary, said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have cut the rate less than expected to give itself leeway to continue cuts in coming months. The commentary also suggested that the Bank of Israel may be trying to postpone the day when people will prefer to invest in dollars rather than shekels. Because interest is rising abroad and falling here, shekel accounts are becoming less attractive, Globes said. \par \par \uc2  Bank Governor Jacob Frenkel is going to the annual meeting of the International Monetary Fund in a few days and prefers to appear at international forums as someone who is keeping the monetary reins short and not buckling under to pressure to quickly lower the interest rate, the commentary said. \par \par \uc2  --The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  more heavily than usual in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading on Tuesday to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket exchange rate after it fell below the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's appointed lower band. The bank bought tens of millions of dollars, Globes reported. \par \par \uc2  -- Economists at Israel Discount Bank expect the budget deficit to reach 12 billion shekels to 13 billion shekels, or 4% of gross domestic product. The original budget shortfall was targeted at 7.6 billion shekels; the deficit currently stands at 8.6 billion shekels. \par \par \uc2  - Amy Teibel \par \par \uc2  (END) DOW JONES EMERGING MARKETS REPORT 25-09-96 \par \par \uc2  0843GMT \par \par \uc2  1802 \par \par \uc2 Dokument em00000020011013ds9p00dhm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
