\par\par \b \uc2  Israel/Discount Cut-3: Tight Policy to Continue? \b0\par\par\uc2 304 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 1996\par 19:08\par \uc2  Emerging Markets Report \par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1996, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Ptachia Bar Shavit, the chief economist at Bank Hapoalim, said the ``minor reduction'' reinforces the conclusion that monetary policy will continue to be very tight through most of 1997. \par \par \uc2  ``A substantial interest rate cut is not expected in the near future'' - even if the parliament decides to slash the 1997 budget by the proposed 4.9 billion shekels, because Frenkel will wait to see to what extent the cuts are implemented, Bar Shavit said. \par \par \uc2  The relatively low rise in the consumer price index in August and the 0.1% rise in the M1 supply in August had created expectations of a deeper rate cut. So too had the appreciation of the shekel, which several weeks ago forced the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket exchange rate above the bank's appointed lowest level. \par \par \uc2  But Bufman, one of the lone voices to predict a three-tenths of a percentage point cut, said the low CPI increase in August was not an indicator of good things to come because the elements that contributed to the low rise will not carry over in the coming months. \par \par \uc2  The August CPI figure was low in part because the shekel appreciated very sharply from early June to late August, reaching the limit of appreciation the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set and forcing the bank to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading, Bufman said. Because the shekel cannot appreciate any further, that factor will not affect prices for the next few months, Bufman said. \par \par \uc2  Furthermore, seasonal elements (such as lower produce and clothing prices) slowed down inflation during the summer and those elements are not going to continue to do so in the coming months, he said. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) DOW JONES EMERGING MARKETS REPORT 24-09-96 \par \par \uc2  1708GMT \par \par \uc2  1802 \par \par \uc2 Dokument em00000020011013ds9o00ki8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
