\par\par \b \uc2  Israel News: Renewed Israel-Syria Talks On Washington Agenda \b0\par\par\uc2 360 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 1996\par 14:27\par \uc2  Emerging Markets Report \par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1996, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TEL AVIV (Dow Jones)--The following news summary is based on Tuesday editions of the Ha'aretz, Yediot Ahronot, Ma'ariv and Jerusalem Post national dailies and the Globes financial gazette: \par \par \uc2  -- Efforts to renew negotiations between Syria and Israel received prominent converage. Breaking the months-old stalemate is the focus of Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu's talks with U.S. officials in Washington this week. Ha'aretz reported that Syria's greater flexibility is likely to permit the renewal of talks before the U.S. presidential election; the Jerusalem Post cited Netanyahu as saying that he was not sure Syria wanted to talk. \par \par \uc2  -- Papers also gave prominent play to Ehud Barak's announcement that he will stand for Labor Party leader. Current party leader Shimon Peres was defeated by Netanyahu in the May elections. Barak is a former military commander and foreign minister and currently serves as a parliament member from the Labor Party. \par \par \uc2  -- An organizer of the Cairo regional economic summit, World Economic Forum director Gregory Blatt, says Egypt will give the go-ahead for the conference within a week. Egypt has threatened to call off the Nov.12-14 conference if Israel does not move faster on peacemaking. \par \par \uc2  -- Provident fund holders withdrew a record 2.2 billion shekels in August -- a 140% jump over July, the Finance Ministry reported on Monday. In the first eight months of the year, holders of the long-term savings plans have withdrawn 7.4 billion shekels. \par \par \uc2  -- The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading on Monday to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket exchange rate from remaining below its lowest set value. According to Ha'aretz, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought tens of millions of dollars, the largest sum it has purchased since it first began \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market two weeks ago. \par \par \uc2  -- A committee on promoting capital investments will weigh the possibility of continuing to let U.S. pension funds enjoy tax breaks, Ha'aretz reported. The panel had recommended that Israel rescind the tax breaks it gives foreign investors. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) DOW JONES EMERGING MARKETS REPORT 10-09-96 \par \par \uc2  1227GMT \par \par \uc2  1802 \par \par \uc2 Dokument em00000020011013ds9a00fuq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
