\par\par \b \uc2  Bank of Israel won't change shekel trading bands. \b0\par\par\uc2 341 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 August 1996\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JERUSALEM, Aug 29 (Reuter) - The Bank of Israel has no intention of changing the present trading band within which the shekel is allowed to fluctuate, sources at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said. \par \par \uc2  The officials said that at this stage there is no intention of enlarging or abolishing the trading band. \par \par \uc2  "There are no talks about this and for sure this won't happen tomorrow, the day after, in another week or in another month," one official said. "Of course we have thoughts about the long term, but there is nothing on the agenda at the moment." \par \par \uc2  The shekel can fluctuate in a trading band of 7.0 percent above or below a slope designed to devalue the Israeli \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at six percent a year against a basket of key \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  If the shekel-basket exchange rate reaches the lower trading band the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  by buying foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in trading on Tuesday and Wednesday by buying small amounts of dollars to stop the shekel-basket exchange rate from falling below the bottom of the band. \par \par \uc2  The officials said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wanted only to preserve the band and had no intention of depreciating the shekel by making massive \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading. \par \par \uc2  They said that all changes in the parameters of the band need to be done with the approval of the Treasury and they doubted the Treasury would approve such a move with the rate being so close to the bottom of the band. \par \par \uc2  Widening the trading band would allow the shekel to appreciate further, if the current trend continues, and cause even further damage to exporters, they said. \par \par \uc2  At the end of May last year, Treasury heads decided to enlarge the band from 5.0 percent above and below the slope to 7.0 percent. But when they did this the shekel-basket exchange rate was at the middle of the band and not at its bottom, the officials said. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011018ds8t03ozo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
