\par\par \b \uc2  PRESS DIGEST - Israel - Aug 28. \b0\par\par\uc2 323 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 August 1996\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JERUSALEM, Aug 28 (Reuter) - These are some of the leading stories in Israeli newspapers on Wednesday. Reuters has not verified these stories and does not vouch for their accuracy. \par \par \uc2  HAARETZ \par \par \uc2  - High alert over warnings about Islamic Jihad intention to carry out spectacular terror attack. \par \par \uc2  - U.S. Senator Spector left for Syria, will pass Israeli message to Syrian President Assad. \par \par \uc2  - Prime Minister Netanyahu: The stock market will go up once it becomes clear how serious we are about privatisation and cutting the budget. \par \par \uc2  - Netanyahu: No special signs of Syrian movement of forces or heightened alert. \par \par \uc2  - Infrastructure Minister Sharon decides to build railway line between Israel's Ashdod port and Erez border crossing to Gaza. \par \par \uc2  - Syrian trade minister: Peace process could get off track unless solution found to economic distress. \par \par \uc2  - Jerusalem municipality demolishes Palestinian structure. \par \par \uc2  YEDIOTH AHRONOTH \par \par \uc2  - Justice Barak responds to invitation for talks with Ultra-Orthodox. \par \par \uc2  - Security sources: Syrian army movements not offensive. \par \par \uc2  - Bank of Israel works to prevent deterioration of dollar. \par \par \uc2  - \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket reaches red line. \par \par \uc2  JERUSALEM POST \par \par \uc2  - Syria prepared to resume peace talks. \par \par \uc2  - Justice Barak receives telephone death threats. \par \par \uc2  - Palestinians protest demolition of illegally built club for disabled. \par \par \uc2  - Shekel appreciates to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's limit. \par \par \uc2  - PM's office: Public options plan for shares in 