\par\par \b \uc2  Israeli shekel gains,  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  buys dollars. \b0\par\par\uc2 178 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 August 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TEL AVIV, Aug 2 (Reuter) - The Israeli shekel gained slightly against the basket of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the dollar because of a local trend of selling foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Despite the intense supply of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in trade only after it had set the representative exchange rates. If the dollar hadn't gained sharply in the world, the shekel would have closed much stronger against the dollar," said Israel Sapir of Bank Leumi. \par \par \uc2  The representative rate for the dollar was 2.9940 shekels, compared with 2.9980 on Tuesday. The basket, which is composed of the dollar, mark, sterling, French franc and yen, traded at 3.4847 shekels against 3.4981 on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  On the crosses the shekel traded against the yen at 3.3248 shekels per 100 yen against 3.3933 on Tuesday and against the mark at 2.1520 shekels per mark from 2.1662 on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  In late afternoon the shekel traded at 2.9940 shekels per dollar. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr8205b4e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
